/**
 * @defgroup pipe_sizing_calculator Pipe Sizing Calculator
 * @ingroup compressed_air_assessments
 * @brief Sizes compressed air distribution pipes to meet a target design velocity.
 * @details This calculator determines the minimum internal cross-sectional area and
 *          corresponding pipe diameter required to carry a given compressed air flow
 *          at or below a specified design velocity. All formulas use U.S. customary units.
 *
 * Correct pipe sizing is critical for efficient compressed air distribution:
 *
 * - **Undersized pipes** create excessive pressure drop, wasting energy and reducing
 *   available pressure at end-use equipment.
 * - **Oversized pipes** increase capital cost and reduce air velocity, which can allow
 *   condensate to accumulate rather than being carried to drain points.
 * - **Main headers** are typically designed for 20 ft/s; velocity should never exceed
 *   30 ft/s in any distribution segment.
 *
 * The calculation applies Boyle's Law to convert the free-air volumetric flow rate
 * to the compressed-air flow at operating pressure, then uses the continuity equation
 * to find the pipe area that limits velocity to the design value.
 *
 * Relevant formulas are documented below.
 *
 * @heading{Cross-Sectional Area}
 * @copydoc pipe_sizing_area_formula
 *
 * @heading{Pipe Diameter}
 * @copydoc pipe_sizing_diameter_formula
 */

/**
 * @defgroup pipe_sizing_area_formula Pipe Cross-Sectional Area Formula
 * @ingroup pipe_sizing_calculator
 * @brief Minimum internal pipe area to limit compressed-air velocity to the design value.
 * @details The formula derives from the continuity equation applied to a compressible
 *          fluid. The free-air flow rate @math{Q} is first converted to the actual
 *          compressed-air volumetric flow at operating pressure using Boyle's Law:
 *
 * @formula{pipe-sizing-qcompressed;
 *   Q_{compressed} = Q \cdot \dfrac{P_{atm}}{P_{line} + P_{atm}}
 * }
 *
 * The required cross-sectional area at the design velocity is:
 *
 * @formula{pipe-sizing-area-ft2;
 *   A_{ft^2} = \dfrac{Q_{compressed}}{v \cdot k_{60}}
 * }
 *
 * Converting from ft² to in² by multiplying by @math{k_{144}}:
 *
 * @formula{pipe-sizing-area;
 *   A = \frac{k_{144} \cdot Q \cdot P_{atm}}{v \cdot k_{60} \cdot (P_{line} + P_{atm})}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{A; Minimum required internal cross-sectional area; \inch\squared}
 * @symrow{k_{144}; Square inches per square foot (144); \inch\squared\per\squareFoot}
 * @symrow{Q; Volumetric free-air flow rate; \cubicFoot\per\minute}
 * @symrow{P_{atm}; Atmospheric (absolute) pressure; \psia}
 * @symrow{v; Maximum allowable compressed-air design velocity; \foot\per\second}
 * @symrow{k_{60}; Seconds per minute (60); \second\per\minute}
 * @symrow{P_{line}; Operating gauge pressure in the pipe; \psig}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @note @math{P_{line} + P_{atm}} converts gauge pressure to absolute pressure
 *       (Boyle's Law requires absolute pressures).
 *       The combined factor @math{k_{144} / k_{60} = 2.4} converts the area from
 *       ft²·min/s to in².
 */

/**
 * @defgroup pipe_sizing_diameter_formula Pipe Diameter Formula
 * @ingroup pipe_sizing_calculator
 * @brief Internal pipe diameter corresponding to the computed cross-sectional area.
 * @details Assumes a circular pipe cross-section. The relationship between internal
 *          diameter and cross-sectional area for a circle is:
 *
 * @math{A = k_c \cdot D^2}
 *
 * Solving for the diameter:
 *
 * @formula{pipe-sizing-diameter;
 *   D = \sqrt{\frac{A}{k_c}}
 * }
 *
 * @subheading{Symbols}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{D; Minimum required internal pipe diameter; \inch}
 * @symrow{A; Cross-sectional area (from \eqref{eq:pipe-sizing-area}); \inch\squared}
 * @symrow{k_c; Circle area factor (0.78 \approx \pi/4); \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @note The factor @math{k_c = 0.78} is an industry-standard rounded approximation
 *       of @math{\pi / 4 \approx 0.7854} used in U.S. customary compressed air
 *       pipe sizing practice. The resulting diameter is the minimum internal
 *       (bore) diameter; the next larger standard nominal pipe size should be
 *       selected for the actual installation.
 */
